{"id": "sevt_01WBgaF7rD5KN43WtKCpzaVX", "content": [{"text": "**Evidence**\n\n- **Deploy lines up.** `a3f9c21` (checkout, \"refactor order summary builder for readability\") shipped **14:31:18**. Checkout p99 jumps **80ms \u2192 ~3,800ms+ at 14:31**, the same minute.\n- **The diff is a batch\u2192per-row regression** (the #1 thing on our burned-us list). `build_summary` used to do one batched query \u2014 `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` grouped in memory. It now loops per order: `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)`, one query per row (classic N+1).\n- **Log confirms it.** First per-row `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` appears at **14:31:18.496** \u2014 right at deploy. A single request (`req_efcce8808bfe`) fires **13** of these queries where it used to fire one.\n- **Mechanism \u2192 pool exhaustion.** `db_pool_utilization` climbs from ~0.2 at 14:31 and pins at **1.0 (100%) by 14:40**. The 500s start exactly then (14:40\u219214:59, 70 of them) and all read `error=PoolTimeout` on `/api/checkout/validate`. Latency rose first (14:31); 500s followed once the pool saturated (14:40).\n\n**Fix:** revert to the batched `order_id__in` query (restore the in-memory grouping).\n\n> **Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 replaced the batched `order_items` lookup with a per-order query (N+1), exhausting checkout's DB connection pool and turning requests into PoolTimeout 500s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:39:51.044173Z", "type": "agent.message"}
